  <data name="rtbCode.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dim firstNumber As Double = 10
Dim secondNumber As Double = 5
Dim result As Double

Select Case True
    Case rbAdd.Checked
        result = firstNumber + secondNumber

    Case rbSubtract.Checked
        result = firstNumber - secondNumber

    Case rbMultiply.Checked
        result = firstNumber * secondNumber

    Case rbDivide.Checked
        If secondNumber = 0 Then
            MessageBox.Show("Cannot divide by zero.")
            Exit Sub
        End If

        result = firstNumber / secondNumber
End Select

lblResult.Text = result.ToString()</value>
  </data>
  <data name="rtbExplanation.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Data Handling in Visual Basic .NET

Data handling refers to the process of receiving, storing, processing, and displaying data in a program.

Variables are used to store data while a program is running. Each variable has a data type that determines what kind of value it can hold.

Common Numeric Data Types:

• Integer — Stores whole numbers.
• Double — Stores numbers with decimal values and provides double-precision floating-point representation.
• Single — Stores floating-point numbers using less memory than Double, but with less precision.
• Decimal — Stores decimal numbers with high precision, making it useful for financial calculations.

Arithmetic Operators:

• Addition (+) — Adds two numbers.
• Subtraction (-) — Subtracts one number from another.
• Multiplication (*) — Multiplies two numbers.
• Division (/) — Divides one number by another and can produce a fractional result.
• Integer Division (\) — Divides two numbers and produces an integer quotient.

Input Validation:

The TryParse() method checks whether text can be converted into a specified data type. It helps prevent invalid input from being used in calculations.

In this calculator, the user selects a data type, enters two numbers, and chooses an arithmetic operation. The program validates the inputs, performs the selected operation, and displays the result.
